    // index
    "ix.0": "High School · US History Since 1877 · §113.41 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "The Panama Canal, 1903 — a World-Power Question",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into the crisis of 1903, take on a stakeholder’s point of view, and work a real, ill-structured question about power, sovereignty, and progress — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> It is 1903. Should the United States build and control a canal across the Isthmus of Panama — and if so, <i>how</i>, and on <i>whose</i> terms?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Vocabulary, key facts about sea power and the canal race, a jigsaw read, and map work on the isthmus. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect & organize",
    "ix.9": "Benefits–costs concept map, primary sources, competing points of view, and a structured argument. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
    "ix.11": "Meet the problem, take a stakeholder role, investigate, propose & defend a solution, debrief. ~2–4 periods.",
    "ix.12": "⛔ <b>Gate:</b> don’t open the Phase 3 problem until students have finished the surface and deep activities. Meeting the problem too early turns inquiry into guessing.",
    "ix.13": "Big idea & objectives",
    "ix.14": "<b>Big idea:</b> Nations weigh <b>strategic interest, cost, and the rights of others</b> when they decide whether and how to project power — and the choices the United States made in 1903 launched it as a world power while leaving a contested legacy of intervention and sovereignty.",
    "ix.15": "<b>Know:</b> why a canal mattered for trade and naval power, how the Panama route was chosen, and what the 1903 secession and treaty actually did.",
    "ix.16": "<b>Understand:</b> the same event looks profoundly different from each stakeholder’s point of view — expansionist, Colombian, Panamanian, and laborer alike.",
    "ix.17": "<b>Do:</b> run the problem-solving and decision-making process end to end — identify the problem, gather information, list and weigh options, choose and defend a solution, and evaluate it (§113.41(c)(31)(B)).",
    "ix.18": "Standards this unit is aligned to (§113.41)",
